GNOME Phone session
===================

Installing this package changes nothing on its own: no unit is enabled, the
regular GNOME session is untouched, and the gnome-phone extension does not run
in it. You pick the phone session in one of two ways.

With a display manager (GDM)
----------------------------

Choose "GNOME Phone" in the session menu at the login screen. It is GNOME's
own session with GNOME Shell started in the gnome-phone mode
(/usr/share/gnome-shell/modes/gnome-phone.json), which always runs the
gnome-phone@yesman.de extension. To make it the default for automatic login,
set it as the user's session:

  busctl call org.freedesktop.Accounts /org/freedesktop/Accounts/User$(id -u USER) \
      org.freedesktop.Accounts.User SetSession s gnome-phone

Without a display manager
-------------------------

gnome-phone.service starts gnome-session on tty7 as one user, logged in by
systemd (PAM service "login"), with no display manager at all. It ships with
User=user; set your own user first, then switch over:

  systemctl edit gnome-phone.service       # [Service] / User=<your user>
  systemctl disable gdm.service            # or phosh.service, or whatever ran
  systemctl enable gnome-phone.service

The extension must then be enabled for that user, as for any extension
(gnome-extensions enable gnome-phone@yesman.de), or the unit given
"ExecStart=" / "ExecStart=/usr/bin/gnome-session --session=gnome-phone" in the
same drop-in, which runs the extension through the session mode as GDM's
"GNOME Phone" does.

Docking
-------

neo-dock switches between gnome-phone.service and gnome-desktop.service (the
same session, laid out as GNOME's desktop) when an external display is
plugged in or out. It is started by a udev rule that is not active by
default, because on a plug or unplug it starts a session. To use it:

  ln -s /usr/share/gnome-phone-session/71-neo-dock.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
  udevadm control --reload

Device hooks for a plug or unplug go in /etc/neo-dock.d/ (each is run with
"connected" or "disconnected").

Lock screen
-----------

The lock screen's PIN or pattern is the user's own password, checked by
/usr/libexec/gp-auth through the PAM service /etc/pam.d/gnome-phone (from
gnome-phone-daemons). A pattern is the digits 1-9 of its dots in the order
they are joined.
